Fillets: two roundings at the ends of a face too narrow for both, one of
them beside a strip narrower than its radius (mitcad#339): e.g. the two
vertical edges of a window through a wall 3.54 thick rounded with 2, the
window's end split into a strip 0.5 wide on one side and the rest, which
meets the strip at 0.35 degrees. The rounding beside the strip overflowed
it onto the face beyond (0011's edge overflow; at 0.35 degrees OCCT also
takes the two faces for tangent), up to 2 from its edge, across the other
rounding's contact on that face; their ends on the faces at the ends then
crossed ("fillets have too big radiuses"). The stored designs' blend rolls
on the strip's far edge instead, its ball tangent to its other face (the
cylinder 0331 builds), and the other rounding keeps its face.

- ChFi3d_Builder::RollsInsteadOfOverflow, once the stripes are computed:
  a stripe's side on a face it reached across a narrow face of its edge (no
  edge of its spine bounds that face), whose contact there lies nearer to
  another stripe's edge than that stripe's contact on the face (the edge
  of its spine on that face, else the one nearest to its contact), may not
  overflow onto that face (myRollStripes, RollsBefore). The stripe is
  computed again; where that fails, as before.
- Such a stripe's start is sought as an overflow's (StartSolOverflow, not
  StartSol, whose search of the neighbouring faces takes nearly tangent
  ones as tangent): the side that reaches that face rolls on the edge before
  it, also where the faces only nearly continue each other (sharpAt: their
  normals apart by more than 1e-4, as convexAt's tangency) and where it
  passed a sliver on the way (the edge before the sliver, 0013's fallback).
  Two stripes overflowing onto the same face across each other's contacts
  both roll.
